{
  "id": "adoption_safety",
  "version": "1.3.0",
  "depends_on": [],
  "capability_keys": [
    "adoption_safety",
    "regression_veto"
  ],
  "description": "THE AGENT-SIDE ADOPTION SAFETY GATE (Wave 6). The conversation experiment world's judge nets its score (flagged_changed - regressions), so a variant that fixes the flagged turn but breaks one other previously-working turn can still judge 'supported' (+2-1=+1) and reach mark_adopt_ready. This seed closes that hole AS GRAPH DATA the agent can inspect and repair: the dispatched child already reports `regressions` in result.json, the dispatch ingest (domains/experiment_dispatch.py::_ingest_result) mechanically carries it onto the Experiment node, and the rule below VETOES adoption of any Experiment whose own child reported regressions > 0 -- it sets `adopt_denied` (which perform_adoptions honours unconditionally: a denied Experiment is never folded back) plus an honest `adopt_denied_reason` the self_improvement_status reader surfaces over NL ('what are you working on?'). Chosen over a stricter mark_adopt_ready where-clause deliberately: the authored mark_adopt_ready_* bodies stay byte-identical (graph-meta oracle parity with seeds/composer_experiment_graph.json's wants_experiment_adopt_rule is preserved untouched), the veto applies uniformly to every experiment family that reports regressions (today: conversation replay), and an ARC child reports no `regressions` key at all, so the attr is never written there and this rule can never bind an ARC experiment. The veto fires in the same run_rules fixpoint as the judge/mark_adopt_ready rules (the regressions attr is written at ingest, before that tick), so perform_adoptions -- host I/O that only runs between ticks -- always sees the denial before it could adopt. || v1.1.0 (2026-10-10): adopt_denied_on_overridden_verdict -- an adopt-ready Experiment whose outcome a family judge has since overridden away from 'supported' is vetoed (measured: tranche 23 smoke run, adopted=True with outcome='refuted'; scripts/gen_adoption_overridden_verdict_veto.py). || v1.2.0 (2026-10-10, plan P1 judge v2): adopt_denied_on_quality_drop vetoes adoption when the child's ReplyQuality report shows any turn's quality fell or the mean fell (scripts/gen_adoption_quality_veto.py). || v1.3.0 (2026-10-10, plan P5c, human decision: everything is weighed): the absolute regression and quality-drop vetoes are replaced by a weighed net over the child's frame-relative report (fixes, improved, regressions = worse in the quality frame or now failing, lateral = same quality, different text) with AdoptionWeighing exchange rates (scripts/gen_weighed_adoption.py).",
  "microtheory": {
    "name": "adoption_safety",
    "description": "a trial is adopted when what it fixes outweighs what it harms, in the frame it is measured in; the exchange rates are held as data"
  },
  "nodes": [
    {
      "local_id": 0,
      "type": "AdoptionWeighing",
      "attrs": {
        "name": "adoption_weighing",
        "fix_weight": 1.0,
        "gain_weight": 0.5,
        "harm_weight": 1.5,
        "lateral_weight": 0.25,
        "min_net": 1.0,
        "description": "The exchange rates the adoption gate weighs a trial by (plan P5c): a fixed flagged turn (or a declared witness moving the right way) is worth fix_weight; an unflagged turn made better gain_weight; a turn made worse in the quality frame, or a broken fix, costs harm_weight; a lateral change (same quality, different text -- the frame cannot see it) costs lateral_weight. A trial is adopted only if its net reaches min_net."
      }
    }
  ],
